QB.add(32,[{"id":32001,"t":"Electrical Definitions","s":"Which definition of conductance (g) is correct?","o":["1/R – the ability to conduct electric current","The flow of electric charge through a medium","The difference in electric potential energy per unit charge between two points","The opposition to the passage of an electric current","Current multiplied by resistance"],"e":"Voltage (ΔV): difference in electric potential energy per unit charge between two points. Current (I): flow of charge. Resistance (R): opposition to current. Conductance g = 1/R, the ability to conduct current. V = IR.","w":["That is current.","That is voltage.","That is resistance.","That is voltage (Ohm’s law)."],"k":"g = 1/R.","rn":[["","What is conductance (g)?","What is conductance (g)?","Ability to conduct electric current; How easily electricity flows along a path"]]},{"id":32002,"t":"Electrical Definitions","s":"If more ion channels for K⁺ open while the driving voltage stays the same, what happens to K⁺ current, using I = V × g?","o":["It increases, as conductance has risen","It decreases, as resistance has risen","It stays the same, as voltage is unchanged","It reverses direction","It becomes zero"],"e":"I = V × g: multiplying the force on an ion (voltage) by its conductance gives the current that can flow. More open channels means higher conductance, so more current for the same voltage.","w":["More channels lower resistance.","Conductance changed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"More channels → more current."},{"id":32003,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Name the voltage level marked by the dashed line at <b>A</b>, which must be crossed for an action potential to fire.","e":"A is the threshold (about −65 to −50 mV). Once crossed, Na⁺ channel positive feedback gives an all-or-none upstroke.","k":"A = threshold.","img":{"src":"32/ap","cap":"Neuronal membrane potential over time after a stimulus. Some labels removed."},"a":["threshold","threshold potential","threshold voltage"],"rn":[["","What is the threshold voltage?","What is the threshold voltage?","-65 to -50 mV"]]},{"id":32004,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Why can ions not simply cross the plasma membrane?","o":["They can’t diffuse through it; they need open channels","They are too small and pass through the lipids too quickly to be controlled","They are repelled by all membrane proteins","They are always bound to ATP","They are uncharged"],"e":"The membrane is selectively permeable: ions cannot cross by simple diffusion, and ionic permeabilities and conductance depend on ion channels being open.","w":["They can’t pass the lipid.","Channels let them through.","Not.","They are charged."],"k":"Ions need channels.","rn":[["","Ionic permeability (and conductance) depends on what?","Ionic permeability (and conductance) depends on what?","Ion channels being open"],["","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","Electrochemical gradient"],["","What does an ionotropic receptor do?","What does an ionotropic receptor do?","Opens a channel; Changes membrane permeability to ions"]]},{"id":32005,"t":"Electrical Definitions","s":"What is the difference between permeability and conductance?","o":["Permeability: does it get through? Conductance: how fast?","Permeability: how fast? Conductance: whether it gets through","They are identical terms","Permeability applies to water only","Conductance applies only to calcium"],"e":"Two ideas: permeability (does the ion get through?) and conductance (how fast can it get through?). Lots of channels for an ion → high permeability and high conductance.","w":["Swapped.","Different ideas.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Permeability vs conductance.","rn":[["","Permeability vs conductance: what does each ask?","Permeability vs conductance: what does each ask?","Permeability: does the ion get through?; Conductance: how fast?"]]},{"id":32006,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Name the pump that sets up the Na⁺ and K⁺ concentration gradients across the membrane.","e":"The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase builds the gradients; blocked for long, gradients run down and Vm drifts towards 0.","k":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","a":["na+/k+-atpase","na k atpase","sodium potassium pump","na+/k+ atpase","na/k atpase","sodium-potassium atpase","na+-k+ pump","na-k pump","sodium pump"],"rn":[["","What does the cell use to set up the ion gradients?","What does the cell use to set up the ion gradients?","Na+-K+ ATPase (pump); Uses ATP"],["","What is the job of the Na+-K+ ATPase at rest?","What is the job of the Na+-K+ ATPase at rest?","Constantly maintain the ion gradients; Uses ATP"]]},{"id":32007,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"A membrane becomes fully permeable to all ions. What happens to Vm?","o":["It becomes about zero","It becomes −90 mV, the equilibrium potential for K⁺","It becomes +60 mV","It oscillates","It becomes −15 mV"],"e":"Fully permeable membranes will generally have a Vm of zero (e.g. death): ions just scoot across and no voltage difference can be generated.","w":["That needs only K⁺ channels.","That needs only Na⁺ channels.","Not.","That is equal Na⁺/K⁺ only."],"k":"Fully permeable → 0 mV.","rn":[["","What is the Vm of a fully permeable membrane?","What is the Vm of a fully permeable membrane?","Zero (generally); e.g. death"]]},{"id":32008,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about where Vm is measured is correct?","o":["A thin layer under the inner membrane face, relative to outside","The whole inside of the cell compared with the whole outside, as the cytoplasm becomes negative","Only the extracellular face","The nucleus relative to the cytoplasm","Between two adjacent neurons"],"e":"Vm is described in terms of the intracellular face. It is not the whole inside of the cell becoming negative, but a thin layer just under the intracellular side relative to the extracellular side (taken as zero).","w":["Not the whole inside.","Intracellular face.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Vm: thin inner layer.","rn":[["","Does the whole inside of the cell become negative at rest?","Does the whole inside of the cell become negative at rest?","No; Only a thin layer under the inner membrane"]]},{"id":32009,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"When the inside of the membrane is positive relative to the outside, how is Vm described?","o":["Positive","Negative, as the outside is reference","Zero","Hyperpolarised","At rest"],"e":"Membrane voltage is described from the intracellular face: when the inside is positive with respect to the extracellular face, “the membrane potential is positive”.","w":["Inside is reference face.","Not.","More negative.","Rest is negative."],"k":"Inside positive = positive Vm.","rn":[["","When do we say \"the membrane potential is positive\"?","When do we say \"the membrane potential is positive\"?","Inside of membrane positive; Relative to the extracellular face"]]},{"id":32010,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"When K⁺ channels open, which way does K⁺ move and what happens to the inside of the membrane?","o":["K⁺ moves out; inside becomes more negative","K⁺ moves in; inside becomes more positive","K⁺ moves out; inside becomes more positive","K⁺ moves in; inside becomes more negative","No movement"],"e":"[K⁺] is higher inside (roughly 120 mM), so when channels open K⁺ flows out and the membrane becomes negative inside. [Na⁺] is higher outside, so Na⁺ flows in and the inside becomes positive.","w":["That is Na⁺.","Losing positive charge makes it negative.","Not.","It moves."],"k":"K⁺ out → negative.","rn":[["","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Out of the cell; Inside becomes negative"],["","Depolarisation","Depolarisation","Inside voltage becomes less negative (more positive)"]]},{"id":32011,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about sodium is correct?","o":["Higher outside; flows in when channels open","Higher inside the cell; flows out when channels open, making the inside more negative","Equal on both sides","Higher outside; flows out","Higher inside; flows in"],"e":"[Na⁺] is higher outside (roughly 120–150 mM), so when channels open Na⁺ flows in and the inside tends to become positive.","w":["That is K⁺.","Not.","It flows in.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ higher outside, flows in.","rn":[["","Is [Na+] higher inside or outside the cell?","Is [Na+] higher inside or outside the cell?","Outside"],["","Is [K+] higher inside or outside the cell?","Is [K+] higher inside or outside the cell?","Inside"]]},{"id":32012,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"What does it mean that many Na⁺ and K⁺ channels are “gated by voltage”?","o":["They open in response to a change in Vm","They open only when a neurotransmitter binds to them","They are always open","They are opened by ATP","They open only at 0 mV"],"e":"Gated means they only open in response to a change in Vm – they sense the Vm. A voltage-gated Na⁺ channel is not really open at rest; when Vm reaches a certain voltage, it opens.","w":["That is ligand-gated.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Voltage-gated: sense Vm.","rn":[["","What does \"gated\" mean for an ion channel?","What does \"gated\" mean for an ion channel?","Only opens in response to a change in Vm; Must reach a certain voltage"]]},{"id":32013,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Which set of equilibrium potentials is correct?","o":["ENa +60; EK −90; ECa +123; ECl −40 (−65 in neurons)","ENa −90; EK +60; ECa +123; ECl −40","ENa +60; EK −90; ECa −123; ECl +40","ENa +123; EK −40; ECa +60; ECl −90","All are zero"],"e":"ENa +60 mV; EK −90 mV; ECa +123 mV; ECl −40 mV (−65 mV in neurons). “Important to know these potentials!” Books vary slightly.","w":["Na⁺ and K⁺ swapped.","Signs wrong.","Mixed up.","Not."],"k":"E values.","rn":[["","What are the equilibrium potentials?","What are the equilibrium potentials?","ENa: +60 mV; EK: -90 mV; ECa: +123 mV; ECl: -40 mV (-65 in neurons)"]]},{"id":32014,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Why does K⁺ stop moving out at −90 mV when its channels are open?","o":["The negative inside pulls K⁺ back, balancing the gradient","All of the K⁺ has left the cell, so there is nothing left to move","The K⁺ channels close at −90 mV","The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase stops","K⁺ becomes uncharged"],"e":"As K⁺ leaves, the concentration gradient dissipates and each K⁺ takes a positive charge, so the inside gets more negative. A positive ion likes negative, so the pull back inside grows until net movement is zero – K⁺’s equilibrium potential, −90 mV. It is a dynamic equilibrium.","w":["Not all leaves.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"EK: dynamic equilibrium.","rn":[["","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","Concentration gradient pushing out; Negative charge inside pulling back"]]},{"id":32015,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Is ion movement frozen at the equilibrium potential?","o":["No – ions still cross both ways; net movement is zero","Yes – all ion movement stops completely at equilibrium","Yes – channels close","No – only Na⁺ moves","No – ions all leave"],"e":"It is a dynamic equilibrium: ions are not frozen; they still cross both ways, but the net movement is nothing.","w":["Not frozen.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dynamic equilibrium.","rn":[["","At equilibrium potential, do ions stop crossing completely?","At equilibrium potential, do ions stop crossing completely?","No; Still cross, but no net movement"],["","At equilibrium potential, what is the net movement of the ion?","At equilibrium potential, what is the net movement of the ion?","Zero; (Dynamic equilibrium)"]]},{"id":32016,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"What drives K⁺ out of the cell initially and what eventually limits it?","o":["The concentration gradient, then charge","Charge first, then the concentration gradient takes over and stops movement","ATP, then calcium","Temperature, then pH","Only the concentration gradient"],"e":"Both drive it – one electrochemical gradient. The steep chemical (concentration) gradient gives the initial momentum, then charge takes over (the Black Friday crowd analogy).","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Both."],"k":"Concentration then charge.","rn":[["","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","Concentration gradient pushing out; Negative charge inside pulling back"]]},{"id":32017,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"What determines how strongly Vm is driven towards a particular ion’s equilibrium potential?","o":["The permeability (conductance) for that ion","The size of the ion relative to the channel pore","The ion’s concentration inside only","The number of vesicles","The ATP level only"],"e":"The greater the permeability (or conductance) for an ion, the more Vm is driven towards that ion’s equilibrium potential. If one channel type is much more conductive than all others, Vm becomes (nearly) that ion’s E.","w":["Not.","Gradient both sides.","Not.","Not."],"k":"More conductance → closer to E.","rn":[["","Greater permeability (conductance) for an ion has what effect?","Greater permeability (conductance) for an ion has what effect?","Vm driven more toward that ion's E"]]},{"id":32018,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"A membrane has many open Na⁺ channels and no other currents. Towards what value (mV) will Vm move?","e":"ENa ≈ +60 mV; EK ≈ −90; ECa ≈ +123; ECl ≈ −40 (−65 in neurons).","k":"+60 mV.","a":["+60","60","+60 mv","60 mv"],"rn":[["","What are the equilibrium potentials?","What are the equilibrium potentials?","ENa: +60 mV; EK: -90 mV; ECa: +123 mV; ECl: -40 mV (-65 in neurons)"]]},{"id":32019,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"A membrane is exactly equally permeable to Na⁺ and K⁺ only. What is Vm?","o":["About −15 mV","About −90 mV, as K⁺ always dominates","About +60 mV","About −70 mV","About 0 mV"],"e":"With both open and equal permeability, Vm is the average of their equilibrium potentials: (+60 + −90)/2 = −15 mV (unlikely in reality).","w":["Not with equal permeability.","Not.","Real neurons.","Not."],"k":"Equal → −15 mV.","rn":[["","Is equal permeability to Na+ and K+ likely?","Is equal permeability to Na+ and K+ likely?","No (unlikely)"]]},{"id":32020,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"A real neuron rests near −70 mV, not −15 mV. What does this tell you?","o":["The membrane is more permeable to K⁺ than Na⁺","The membrane is more permeable to Na⁺ than K⁺, pulling it negative","The membrane is impermeable to both","Ca²⁺ dominates","Cl⁻ dominates"],"e":"A real neuron measures about −70 mV, more negative than −15. So the membrane is more permeable to potassium, which is in fact the case.","w":["Na⁺ would make it positive.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Rest: K⁺ dominates.","rn":[["","Measured resting Vm is ~-70, not -15 mV: what does this tell you?","Measured resting Vm is ~-70, not -15 mV: what does this tell you?","Membrane more permeable to K+ than Na+"]]},{"id":32021,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Which three components make up the resting membrane potential?","o":["Na⁺ diffusion, K⁺ diffusion, Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","Ca²⁺ diffusion, Cl⁻ diffusion and voltage-gated Na⁺ channels","Ligand-gated channels only","Neurotransmitter release","Gap junctions"],"e":"Resting membrane potential (~−90 mV in neurons) is made up of the sodium diffusion potential, the potassium diffusion potential and the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase. Ranges of −70 to −90 mV are quoted.","w":["Not listed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Resting Vm components.","rn":[["","What are the 3 contributors to the resting membrane potential?","What are the 3 contributors to the resting membrane potential?","Sodium diffusion potential; Potassium diffusion potential; Na+-K+ ATPase"]]},{"id":32022,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"How much does the Na⁺/K⁺ pump contribute directly to the resting potential, and why?","o":["About −4 mV, as it moves 3 Na⁺ out per 2 K⁺ in","About −40 mV, as it moves 3 K⁺ in for every 2 Na⁺ out","About +4 mV","Nothing","About −86 mV"],"e":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump pumps 3 Na⁺ out for every 2 K⁺ in – a continual loss of positive charge – adding about −4 mV (electrogenic). Diffusion alone gives about −86 mV; total −90 mV.","w":["Wrong numbers.","Negative.","It adds.","That is diffusion."],"k":"Pump: −4 mV.","rn":[["","How many Na+ and K+ ions does the Na+-K+ pump move per cycle?","How many Na+ and K+ ions does the Na+-K+ pump move per cycle?","3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in"]]},{"id":32023,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Why does the Goldman equation give about −86 mV, close to EK?","o":["The membrane is ~100× more permeable to K⁺ than Na⁺","The membrane is ~100× more permeable to Na⁺ than K⁺","The pump adds −86 mV","Ca²⁺ dominates","There are no leak channels"],"e":"There is slight Na⁺ permeability through K⁺–Na⁺ leak channels, but the membrane is about 100 times more permeable to K⁺ than Na⁺, so the Goldman equation gives −86 mV, near the K⁺ potential.","w":["Reversed.","Pump −4.","Not.","There are."],"k":"100× K⁺ permeability.","rn":[["","Resting nerve membrane: what ratio of K+ to Na+ permeability gives -86 mV?","Resting nerve membrane: what ratio of K+ to Na+ permeability gives -86 mV?","K+ about 100 times Na+"],["","How much more permeable is the nerve membrane to K+ than Na+?","How much more permeable is the nerve membrane to K+ than Na+?","~100 times"]]},{"id":32024,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"What range should you give if asked the resting potential of a neuron?","o":["Roughly −70 to −90 mV","Exactly −70 mV, as books all agree","+20 to +40 mV","−15 mV","0 mV"],"e":"Learn the ranges; books vary. If asked, say roughly between −70 and −90 mV (“closer to 70”). “Welcome to science. We never give exact numbers.”","w":["Books vary.","AP peak.","Equal permeability.","Fully permeable."],"k":"Rest: −70 to −90.","rn":[["","Roughly what is intracellular [K+]?","Roughly what is intracellular [K+]?","~120 mM"]]},{"id":32025,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which statement compares rest with the action potential correctly?","o":["Rest: K⁺-permeable, −70 to −90; AP: Na⁺/Ca²⁺-permeable, +20 to +40","Rest: Na⁺-permeable, +20 to +40; AP: K⁺-permeable, −70 to −90","Both are K⁺-permeable","Both are 0 mV","AP: Cl⁻-permeable"],"e":"Rest: Vm −70 to −90 mV, membrane more permeable to K⁺. AP: Vm +20 to +40 mV, membrane temporarily more permeable to Na⁺, Ca²⁺ or both.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Rest vs AP.","rn":[["","At rest, Vm and the membrane is more permeable to?","At rest, Vm and the membrane is more permeable to?","-70 to -90 mV; K+"]]},{"id":32026,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which sequence matches “what really happens” in an action potential?","o":["K⁺ clamps negative → depolarise to threshold → Na⁺ in → K⁺ out","Na⁺ clamps negative → depolarise → K⁺ in → Na⁺ out","Threshold → K⁺ out → Na⁺ in → rest","Na⁺ out → K⁺ in → threshold","Ca²⁺ in → Cl⁻ out → rest"],"e":"(1) Rest: K⁺ leaving clamps Vm negative (near EK). (2) An external factor depolarises slightly; if threshold is reached, (3) Na⁺ conductance shoots up, Na⁺ enters, depolarisation. (4) After a delay Na⁺ conduction falls, K⁺ conductance rises, K⁺ leaves – repolarisation.","w":["K⁺ clamps.","Na⁺ before K⁺.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AP sequence.","rn":[["","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","K+ going out of the cell; Near EK"]]},{"id":32027,"t":"Action Potential","s":"What is a “failed initiation”?","o":["A depolarisation below threshold, pulled back by K⁺","An action potential that reaches the peak but fails to repolarise","An AP during the absolute refractory period","A neurotransmitter not released","A blocked Na⁺/K⁺ pump"],"e":"If Vm remains below threshold, background K⁺ permeability pulls Vm back to rest – a failed initiation.","w":["Not.","None happens then.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Failed initiation.","rn":[["","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Background K+ permeability pulls Vm back to rest; \"Failed initiation\""]]},{"id":32028,"t":"Action Potential","s":"What is the threshold voltage range?","o":["−65 to −50 mV","−90 to −70 mV","+20 to +40 mV","0 to +60 mV","−15 mV"],"e":"When the voltage goes above the threshold voltage (−65 to −50 mV), the cell is committed to an AP – all-or-none.","w":["Rest.","AP peak.","Not.","Equal permeability."],"k":"Threshold: −65 to −50.","rn":[["","What is the threshold voltage?","What is the threshold voltage?","-65 to -50 mV"]]},{"id":32029,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why is the action potential described as “all-or-none”?","o":["Once threshold is crossed, positive feedback commits it","The amplitude grows with stimulus size, up to a maximum","It needs all channels open at rest","It occurs in all neurons at once","It never fails"],"e":"A few Na⁺ channels open → Na⁺ in → more depolarisation → more Na⁺ channels open: positive feedback. Above threshold the cell is committed to an AP; it happens fully or not at all.","w":["Not graded.","Closed at rest.","Not.","Below threshold it fails."],"k":"All-or-none via positive feedback.","rn":[["","What happens once Vm goes above threshold?","What happens once Vm goes above threshold?","Cell committed to an AP"],["","The Na+ channel opening and depolarisation cycle is what type of loop?","The Na+ channel opening and depolarisation cycle is what type of loop?","Positive feedback"]]},{"id":32030,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which two delayed events cause repolarisation?","o":["Na⁺ channel inactivation and delayed rectifier K⁺ opening","Na⁺ channel opening and K⁺ channel inactivation","Ca²⁺ channel opening and Cl⁻ influx","Pump inhibition and leak closure","Neurotransmitter release and reuptake"],"e":"Due to the passage of time, 2 delayed-action events occur: Na⁺ channel inactivation (↓ Na⁺ current in) and opening of delayed rectifier K⁺ channels (↑ K⁺ out).","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Repolarisation: Na⁺ inactivation + delayed K⁺.","rn":[["","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","Na+ channel inactivation; Delayed rectifier K+ channels open"]]},{"id":32031,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why does Vm undershoot below the resting level at <b>D</b>?","o":["Slow K⁺ channels let lots of K⁺ out","Na⁺ channels stay open longer than K⁺ channels, so more Na⁺ enters","Ca²⁺ enters","The pump stops","Cl⁻ leaves"],"e":"K⁺ channels are slow and sluggish (“haven’t had their coffee”), while Na⁺ channels open and close with “military precision”. The slowness lets lots of K⁺ out, so Vm plummets past rest, approaching EK – the after-hyperpolarisation.","w":["Na⁺ channels close quickly.","Not.","It works hard.","Not."],"k":"Slow K⁺ → AHP.","img":{"src":"32/ap","cap":"Neuronal membrane potential over time after a stimulus. Some labels removed."},"rn":[["","Na+ channels vs K+ channels: speed of opening?","Na+ channels vs K+ channels: speed of opening?","Na+: fast, open briefly then close; K+: slow, sluggish (\"delayed\")"],["","Why is it useful that K+ channels are slow?","Why is it useful that K+ channels are slow?","Timing: open after Na+ channels"]]},{"id":32032,"t":"Action Potential","s":"No new action potential can be fired, however strong the stimulus, because Na⁺ channels are inactivated. Name this period.","e":"Absolute refractory: Na⁺ channels inactivated; relative: an AP needs a larger stimulus.","k":"Absolute refractory period.","a":["absolute refractory period","absolute refractory","absolute"],"rn":[["","What does AHP cause?","What does AHP cause?","Absolute refractory period"],["","Absolute refractory period","Absolute refractory period","New APs cannot be initiated; Na+ channels inactivated"]]},{"id":32033,"t":"Action Potential","s":"What is the relative refractory period?","o":["An AP is possible only with a higher-than-normal stimulus","An AP cannot be initiated under any stimulus, however strong","The resting state","The upstroke","A failed initiation"],"e":"A relative refractory period is when, with a high enough voltage, an AP might be initiated, but it needs to be higher than normal to activate the resting sodium channels.","w":["Absolute.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Relative: higher stimulus needed.","rn":[["","Relative refractory period","Relative refractory period","AP possible with high enough voltage; Must be higher than normal"]]},{"id":32034,"t":"Action Potential","s":"During the AHP, what are the permeabilities?","o":["↑ K⁺ and ↓ Na⁺","↑ Na⁺ and ↓ K⁺, pushing Vm more negative","Both ↑","Both ↓","Only Ca²⁺ ↑"],"e":"During AHP: ↑ K⁺ permeability and ↓ Na⁺ permeability → Vm becomes even more negative.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AHP: ↑K⁺, ↓Na⁺."},{"id":32035,"t":"Action Potential","s":"About how long does a neuronal action potential last (ms)?","e":"A whole action potential lasts about a millisecond.","k":"≈1 ms.","a":["1","1 ms","one","about 1 ms","~1 ms","1ms"],"rn":[["","What does AP stand for?","What does AP stand for?","Action potential"],["","When do K+ channels start opening in an AP (action potential)?","When do K+ channels start opening in an AP (action potential)?","A fraction of a millisecond after Na+ channels"],["","When do K+ channels close again after an AP (action potential)?","When do K+ channels close again after an AP (action potential)?","When Vm returns negative; After a delay of a millisecond or more"]]},{"id":32036,"t":"Action Potential","s":"How does an action potential spread along the axon?","o":["Local current opens nearby Na⁺ channels, giving a wave","The whole inside of the axon depolarises at once along its length","Neurotransmitter diffuses down the axon","Ca²⁺ flows along the axon core","Gap junctions link every patch"],"e":"As Na⁺ rushes in it locally changes Vm; nearby Na⁺ channels sense this and open, then the next patch, via positive feedback – a wave. Only the thin layer just inside the membrane changes, not the whole cell.","w":["Only a thin layer.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Propagation: local currents.","rn":[["","How does an AP spread along the axon?","How does an AP spread along the axon?","Na+ entry changes local Vm; Nearby Na+ channels sense it and open; Wave of depolarisation"]]},{"id":32037,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why do real axons transmit in one direction?","o":["Na⁺ channels behind are inactive for a while","K⁺ channels ahead are permanently closed","Myelin blocks backward flow","Ca²⁺ stops backward flow","They don’t – both directions always"],"e":"After Na⁺ channels open and close they become inactive for a while and do not respond, so the wave can only travel forward, from the cell body down the axon. (An excitable membrane itself has no single direction, per the notes.)","w":["Not.","Not stated.","Not.","Real axons go one way."],"k":"One way: Na⁺ inactivation.","rn":[["","Why do axons transmit in only one direction?","Why do axons transmit in only one direction?","Na+ channels just behind are inactivated; Cannot reopen for a while"]]},{"id":32039,"t":"Action Potential","s":"During an action potential, how do Na⁺ and K⁺ conductance increases compare?","o":["Na⁺ rises several thousand-fold; K⁺ only ~30-fold","K⁺ rises several thousand-fold; Na⁺ only ~30-fold","Both rise 30-fold","Neither changes","Both rise 1000-fold"],"e":"Na⁺ conductance increases several thousand-fold (up to 5000-fold) early in the AP, whereas K⁺ conductance increases only about 30-fold later and for a short time after.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","They change.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ g ↑ thousands; K⁺ ~30×.","rn":[["","Na+ conductance increase during an AP?","Na+ conductance increase during an AP?","Up to ~5000-fold (several thousand)"]]},{"id":32040,"t":"Action Potential","s":"At rest, how does K⁺ conductance compare with Na⁺ conductance?","o":["50 to 100 times greater, due to leak channels","50 to 100 times smaller, due to fewer K⁺ channels","Equal","5000 times greater","Zero"],"e":"At rest, K⁺ conductance is 50 to 100 times Na⁺ conductance, because of much greater K⁺ leakage through the leak channels.","w":["Greater.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Rest: gK 50–100× gNa.","rn":[["","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","50 to 100 times greater; Due to leak channels"]]},{"id":32041,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which statement about chemical and electrical synapses is correct?","o":["Chemical: one-way; electrical: gap junctions, attenuated signal","Chemical: gap junctions; electrical: one-way via neurotransmitters","Both use neurotransmitters","Both are one-way via gap junctions","Electrical synapses pass the full AP unchanged"],"e":"Chemical synapse: one-way conduction; an AP releases neurotransmitter that binds post-synaptic channels. Electrical synapse: neurons physically connected through gap junctions; the AP is attenuated, so the other side gets a blip; enough blips give an AP.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Attenuated."],"k":"Chemical one-way; electrical gap junctions.","rn":[["","What connects neurons at an electrical synapse?","What connects neurons at an electrical synapse?","Gap junctions"],["","What do gap junction channels do at an electrical synapse?","What do gap junction channels do at an electrical synapse?","Directly connect pre- and post-synaptic cells; Let ions pass between them"],["","How does the width of the electrical synapse gap compare with the chemical synapse cleft?","How does the width of the electrical synapse gap compare with the chemical synapse cleft?","Electrical: 20-40 Å; Chemical: 200-300 Å"]]},{"id":32042,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"How is the electrical signal turned into a chemical one at the presynaptic terminal?","o":["Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open; Ca²⁺ triggers release","Voltage-gated Na⁺ influx directly fuses vesicles with the membrane","K⁺ efflux releases vesicles","The pump releases vesicles","Ligand-gated channels release vesicles"],"e":"When Vm becomes positive inside during the AP, calcium channels open; increased Ca²⁺ influx raises cytosolic free calcium, which triggers vesicle fusion and release of the neurotransmitter.","w":["Ca²⁺ triggers release.","Not.","Not.","Postsynaptic."],"k":"Ca²⁺ triggers release.","rn":[["","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Calcium channels; (Voltage-gated Ca2+)"],["","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","Increase in cytosolic free Ca2+"]]},{"id":32043,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Why does Ca²⁺ entry at the terminal add less to depolarisation than Na⁺?","o":["There are fewer, localised Ca²⁺ channels","Ca²⁺ is negatively charged, so it opposes depolarisation","Ca²⁺ leaves rather than enters","Ca²⁺ is pumped out instantly","Ca²⁺ is bound to ATP"],"e":"Yes Ca²⁺ adds to depolarisation, but there are fewer calcium channels than sodium channels, so the rise is not as high. The Ca²⁺ channels are localised where the synapse is, giving a localised pulse whose job is releasing neurotransmitter.","w":["2+.","Enters.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Fewer Ca²⁺ channels.","rn":[["","Why is Ca2+ entry at the terminal not as large as Na+ entry?","Why is Ca2+ entry at the terminal not as large as Na+ entry?","Fewer Ca2+ channels than Na+ channels"],["","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Very localised; Where neurotransmitter is released"]]},{"id":32044,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"What happens to the Ca²⁺ concentration after neurotransmitter release?","o":["It rapidly drops, ending the signal","It stays high until the next action potential arrives","It rises further","It causes another AP","It is released into the cleft"],"e":"After the vesicle merges and releases, the Ca²⁺ concentration rapidly drops, ending the signal.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ drops fast.","rn":[["","What happens to Ca2+ after release?","What happens to Ca2+ after release?","Rapidly drops; Ends the signal"]]},{"id":32045,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Why are drugs that mimic a neurotransmitter not neurotransmitters?","o":["They are exogenous, not made by the neuron","They are made by the neuron but in the wrong place, such as the soma","They act on different receptors","They are always hormones","They never act at synapses"],"e":"A neurotransmitter must be made in the neuron (endogenous), released extracellularly, signal to other cells and work under physiological conditions. Drugs are exogenous; they mimic. Hormones (e.g. adrenaline) can also mimic.","w":["Not.","Same receptors.","Not.","They can."],"k":"Drugs are exogenous.","rn":[["","Why are drugs not neurotransmitters?","Why are drugs not neurotransmitters?","Exogenous; Not made by the neuron"]]},{"id":32046,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Where can neurotransmitters be synthesised?","o":["In the soma or locally in the axon terminal","Only in the synaptic cleft after release from the neuron","Only in the post-synaptic cell","Only in the blood","Only in glial cells"],"e":"Synthesis: in the soma (cell body) or locally in the axon terminal; sometimes instead of synthesis it is recycled after use.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Synthesis: soma or terminal.","rn":[["","Where is a neurotransmitter synthesised?","Where is a neurotransmitter synthesised?","Soma (cell body); Or locally in the axon terminal"]]},{"id":32047,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A transmitter binds an ionotropic receptor (5a). What happens?","o":["A channel opens, changing permeability and possibly Vm","An intracellular cascade via G protein and cAMP begins","The transmitter enters the nucleus","The vesicle refills","Reuptake starts"],"e":"5a ionotropic: channel opens, changing membrane permeability and possibly membrane potential. 5b metabotropic: intracellular cascade (e.g. G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP).","w":["That is metabotropic.","Not.","Not.","Termination."],"k":"Ionotropic: channel opens.","rn":[["","What does an ionotropic receptor do?","What does an ionotropic receptor do?","Opens a channel; Changes membrane permeability to ions"]]},{"id":32048,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which are the routes of signal termination?","o":["Enzyme degradation, reuptake, phosphodiesterase on cAMP","Diffusion into blood and storage in vesicles in the post-synaptic cell","Ca²⁺ influx","Na⁺ influx","Vesicle fusion"],"e":"Termination by removal: 6a degradation by enzymes in the cleft, or 6b recycling by reuptake transporters; 7 termination also by enzymes such as phosphodiesterase degrading postsynaptic molecules like cAMP.","w":["Not.","Release.","Not.","Release."],"k":"Termination routes.","rn":[["","Which enzyme degrades post-synaptic cAMP to end the signal?","Which enzyme degrades post-synaptic cAMP to end the signal?","Phosphodiesterase"]]},{"id":32049,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Why must termination be proportional to the signal?","o":["One impulse should give one cycle, or e.g. walking fails","So the transmitter can accumulate and strengthen later signals","So vesicles are never refilled","So Ca²⁺ stays high","It need not be proportional"],"e":"One nerve impulse → one release → one cycle of receptor binding. If acetylcholine were not cleared you would have a non-proportional signal to muscles and be unable to walk, or find it very challenging. “Super, super important.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","It must."],"k":"Proportional termination."},{"id":32050,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which drugs act on the neurotransmitter life cycle?","o":["Antidepressants, dementia drugs, botox","Antibiotics, antacids and laxatives","Insulin and cortisol","Vaccines","Diuretics only"],"e":"Each step is a good drug target, e.g. antidepressants, drugs for dementia, botox.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Drug targets: life cycle.","rn":[["","Examples of drugs targeting the neurotransmitter life cycle?","Examples of drugs targeting the neurotransmitter life cycle?","Antidepressants; Drugs for dementia; Botox"]]},{"id":32051,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A neurotransmitter opens a channel that lets chloride in. What effect is expected?","o":["Inhibitory","Excitatory – it depolarises the post-synaptic cell","No effect","Neurotransmitter release","An action potential"],"e":"Depending on the transmitter, the effect may be excitatory (depolarises) or inhibitory, e.g. binding a receptor that lets chloride in – the idea of neural control.","w":["Na⁺ would excite.","It acts.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cl⁻ in → inhibitory.","rn":[["","Post-synaptic receptor lets Cl- in: effect?","Post-synaptic receptor lets Cl- in: effect?","Inhibitory"]]},{"id":32052,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which statement is FALSE?","o":["Depolarisation happens when sodium leaves the cell","Potassium is in higher concentration inside the cell","An AP happens if Vm crosses threshold","Repolarisation happens when delayed rectifier K⁺ channels open","During the absolute refractory period, APs cannot be initiated"],"e":"False: depolarisation happens when sodium enters (“definitely false; it doesn’t make any sense at all”). The others are true.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Na⁺ enters to depolarise.","rn":[["","What are 2 causes of the initial depolarisation?","What are 2 causes of the initial depolarisation?","Nearby cell depolarising; Neurotransmitter opening a ligand-gated channel"],["","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","Concentration gradient pushing out; Negative charge inside pulling back"],["","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Out of the cell; Inside becomes negative"]]},{"id":32053,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"“Sodium influx is responsible for depolarisation and for release of the neurotransmitter.” Why is this false?","o":["Ca²⁺ influx triggers release","Na⁺ efflux is responsible for depolarisation, not influx","K⁺ influx triggers release","Neither Na⁺ nor Ca²⁺ is involved","Release is spontaneous"],"e":"False (“It’s rubbish!”): Na⁺ influx causes depolarisation, but it is the Ca²⁺ influx (via voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels) that triggers neurotransmitter release.","w":["Influx.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ triggers release.","rn":[["","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","Increase in cytosolic free Ca2+"]]},{"id":32054,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"“Neurotransmitters are released by cells and circulate in the systemic circulation.” Why is this false?","o":["They are released by neurons to nearby cells","They are released by all cells into the blood but are degraded quickly","They are only in the CNS","They are hormones","They are ions"],"e":"A neurotransmitter is released extracellularly by a neuron to signal to other cells; you would not release acetylcholine into your blood to go round the body – “that would be bad”.","w":["Neurons only.","Periphery too.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Not systemic.","rn":[["","What are the 4 features of a neurotransmitter?","What are the 4 features of a neurotransmitter?","Endogenous chemical; Released extracellularly by a neuron; Signals to neurons, myocytes, endocrine cells; Under physiological conditions"]]},{"id":32055,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"What makes a cell “excitable”?","o":["It can change its voltage and interact with others","It can divide rapidly in response to signals from neighbouring cells","It has no ion channels","It is fully permeable","It produces hormones"],"e":"Excitable cells can change their voltage and interact with other excitable cells. Voltage across the membrane = information.","w":["Not.","Needs channels.","Would be 0 mV.","Not."],"k":"Excitable: voltage changes.","rn":[["","What are excitable cells?","What are excitable cells?","Cells that can change their voltage; And interact with other excitable cells"]]},{"id":32056,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"“Voltage = information” means what?","o":["Changes in Vm carry signals","The resting voltage never changes, so it stores memory","Voltage is measured in bits","Only hormones carry information","Information is in the DNA"],"e":"In excitable cells, voltage across the membrane is used to signal changes; ions are used for signalling and their movements determine voltages and currents.","w":["It changes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Vm changes = information.","rn":[["","In excitable cells, voltage across the membrane equals what?","In excitable cells, voltage across the membrane equals what?","Information; Used to signal changes"]]},{"id":32057,"t":"Action Potential","s":"The small bumps at <b>C</b> rise but never reach A, and the K⁺ current pulls them back. What are they called?","e":"C shows failed initiations: depolarisations below threshold.","k":"C = failed initiations.","img":{"src":"32/ap","cap":"Neuronal membrane potential over time after a stimulus. Some labels removed."},"a":["failed initiations","failed initiation","subthreshold depolarisation","subthreshold depolarization","subthreshold potentials","subthreshold"],"rn":[["","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Background K+ permeability pulls Vm back to rest; \"Failed initiation\""]]},{"id":32059,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about neuronal communication is correct?","o":["Ion currents are essential for neuronal communication","Neurons communicate only electrically, never chemically","Ion channels are never gated","Vm is always zero","Neurotransmitters circulate in blood"],"e":"Key messages: neuronal signalling is essential and involves electrical and chemical signalling; ion currents are essential for neuronal communication.","w":["Both.","Gated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ion currents essential.","rn":[["","What are the key take-home messages of neuronal signalling?","What are the key take-home messages of neuronal signalling?","Essential; electrical and chemical; Ion currents essential for communication"]]},{"id":32060,"t":"Action Potential","s":"In a typical neuron, approximately what is Vm at the peak labelled <b>B</b> (in mV)?","e":"A neuron rests near −70 mV and peaks around +30 mV; the AP approaches but does not reach ENa (+60 mV) because Na⁺ channels inactivate and K⁺ channels open.","k":"≈ +30 mV.","img":{"src":"32/ap","cap":"Neuronal membrane potential over time after a stimulus. Some labels removed."},"a":["+30","30","+30 mv","30 mv","plus 30"],"rn":[["","K+ conductance increase during an AP?","K+ conductance increase during an AP?","~30-fold"],["","Action potential: rest and peak voltages","Action potential: rest and peak voltages","Rest: -70 to -90 mV; Peak: +20 to +40 mV"]]},{"id":32061,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A toxin opens many extra K⁺ channels in a resting neuron. What happens to Vm?","o":["It moves closer to EK (−90 mV)","It moves towards ENa (+60 mV) as more positive ions move","It moves to 0 mV","It triggers an action potential","It stays exactly the same"],"e":"The greater the conductance for an ion, the more Vm is driven towards its equilibrium potential. More open K⁺ channels push Vm closer to EK (−90 mV), making an AP harder to start.","w":["K⁺ leaves, making it negative.","Not fully permeable.","Moves away from threshold.","It changes."],"k":"More K⁺ g → towards EK.","rn":[["","Extra Na+ current entering causes what?","Extra Na+ current entering causes what?","More depolarisation; Vm moves closer to 0 mV"]]},{"id":32062,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A drug blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channels. What would happen to action potentials?","o":["They could not be generated","They would be larger, as K⁺ would not oppose them","They would last longer","Repolarisation would stop","Nothing would change"],"e":"The upstroke needs Na⁺ conductance to shoot up (positive feedback) above threshold. Blocking voltage-gated Na⁺ channels prevents depolarisation, so APs cannot be generated.","w":["No upstroke.","Not.","Not.","It would."],"k":"Na⁺ block → no AP."},{"id":32063,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A drug delays opening of delayed rectifier K⁺ channels even further. What would you predict?","o":["Slower repolarisation, so a longer AP","Faster repolarisation, so a shorter AP","No depolarisation at all","A more negative resting potential","No neurotransmitter release ever"],"e":"Repolarisation depends on Na⁺ channel inactivation and the delayed rectifier K⁺ channels opening. Delaying K⁺ channels further would slow repolarisation and lengthen the AP.","w":["Opposite.","Na⁺ drives depolarisation.","Not.","Not stated."],"k":"Delay K⁺ → longer AP."},{"id":32064,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A stimulus depolarises a neuron from −70 to −60 mV and then stops. If the threshold is −55 mV, what happens?","o":["Failed initiation; Vm returns to rest","A full action potential, since any depolarisation commits the cell","A half-sized action potential","Permanent depolarisation","Absolute refractoriness"],"e":"If Vm remains below threshold, background K⁺ permeability pulls Vm back to rest – a failed initiation. APs are all-or-none: no half-sized APs.","w":["Below threshold.","All-or-none.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Below threshold = fail.","rn":[["","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Background K+ permeability pulls Vm back to rest; \"Failed initiation\""]]},{"id":32065,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A second strong stimulus arrives just after an action potential, during the relative refractory period. What is needed for a new AP?","o":["A larger than normal stimulus","No stimulus – the AP fires automatically","It is impossible","A smaller stimulus","A K⁺ channel blocker"],"e":"Relative refractory period: an AP might be initiated with a high enough voltage, higher than normal, to activate the resting sodium channels. In the absolute period none is possible.","w":["Not.","Absolute.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Relative: bigger stimulus."},{"id":32066,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Extracellular K⁺ is greatly increased, reducing K⁺’s concentration gradient. What happens to the resting Vm?","o":["It becomes less negative","It becomes more negative, as more K⁺ is outside to pull","It stays at −90 mV","It becomes +60 mV","It becomes −15 mV exactly"],"e":"K⁺ leaving down its concentration gradient makes the inside negative. A smaller K⁺ gradient gives less outward driving force, so the resting potential becomes less negative.","w":["Reverse.","It changes.","Not.","Not exactly."],"k":"Less K⁺ gradient → less negative.","rn":[["","Depolarisation","Depolarisation","Inside voltage becomes less negative (more positive)"],["","Repolarisation","Repolarisation","Inside voltage becomes less positive (more negative); Returns to resting potential"],["","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Out of the cell; Inside becomes negative"]]},{"id":32067,"t":"Action Potential","s":"The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is blocked for a long time. What happens over time?","o":["Gradients run down and Vm drifts towards zero","Vm becomes more negative as K⁺ accumulates outside","Vm instantly becomes +60 mV","Action potentials get larger","Nothing, as the pump is not needed"],"e":"The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase constantly puts K⁺ back in and Na⁺ out, maintaining gradients and Vm. Without it gradients dissipate; a membrane without gradients has no voltage.","w":["Not.","Not instantly.","Smaller.","It is needed."],"k":"Pump block → gradients lost."},{"id":32068,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A drug blocks presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels. What happens at the synapse?","o":["Less neurotransmitter is released","More neurotransmitter is released, as Ca²⁺ normally inhibits release","The AP cannot depolarise the terminal","Reuptake stops","The post-synaptic receptors are blocked"],"e":"Ca²⁺ influx through voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels raises cytosolic free Ca²⁺, which triggers vesicle fusion and release. Blocking them reduces release.","w":["Ca²⁺ triggers release.","Na⁺ depolarises.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ block → ↓ release.","rn":[["","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","Increase in cytosolic free Ca2+"],["","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Very localised; Where neurotransmitter is released"]]},{"id":32069,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A drug inhibits the enzyme that breaks down a transmitter in the cleft. What is the effect?","o":["The transmitter stays longer, prolonging the signal","The transmitter is removed faster, shortening the signal","No transmitter is released","The AP is blocked","Ca²⁺ stays high"],"e":"Signal termination by degradation (6a) removes transmitter from the cleft. Blocking the enzyme leaves it longer, giving a non-proportional signal (like ACh not cleared at muscle).","w":["Opposite.","Release unaffected.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Block degradation → prolonged.","rn":[["","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","By enzymes in the synaptic cleft"]]},{"id":32070,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A drug blocks reuptake transporters at a synapse. What happens?","o":["More transmitter stays in the cleft","The transmitter is degraded faster in the post-synaptic cell","Vesicles fill faster","Less transmitter acts","APs stop"],"e":"Recycling into the presynaptic cell (reuptake, 6b) removes transmitter. Blocking reuptake (e.g. some antidepressants) keeps more transmitter in the cleft.","w":["Not.","Less recycled.","More acts.","Not."],"k":"Block reuptake → more in cleft.","rn":[["","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","By enzymes in the synaptic cleft"]]},{"id":32071,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"After a metabotropic receptor is activated, which second messenger is degraded by phosphodiesterase to end the signal?","e":"Termination routes: enzyme degradation, reuptake, and phosphodiesterase breaking down cAMP.","k":"cAMP.","a":["camp","cyclic amp"],"rn":[["","Which enzyme degrades post-synaptic cAMP to end the signal?","Which enzyme degrades post-synaptic cAMP to end the signal?","Phosphodiesterase"]]},{"id":32072,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"An electrical synapse links two neurons. The first fires an AP. What does the second neuron see?","o":["A smaller blip that may summate to an AP","The full AP, unchanged, with no delay at all","Nothing","Neurotransmitter release","Hyperpolarisation only"],"e":"At an electrical synapse neurons are connected by gap junctions. The full AP does not pass through: it is attenuated, so the other side gets a blip; enough blips give an AP.","w":["Attenuated.","It sees a blip.","Chemical.","Not."],"k":"Electrical: attenuated blip."},{"id":32073,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Which change would make the resting Vm more positive?","o":["Opening more Na⁺ channels","Opening more K⁺ channels, letting positive ions leave","Activating the Na⁺/K⁺ pump more","Removing Na⁺ from outside","Opening Cl⁻ channels in a neuron at −65"],"e":"Na⁺ is higher outside; more Na⁺ conductance drives Vm towards ENa (+60), making it more positive. More K⁺ conductance drives it to −90.","w":["More negative.","More negative.","Less Na⁺ gradient.","ECl ≈ rest."],"k":"More Na⁺ g → more positive.","rn":[["","Equilibrium potential (E)","Equilibrium potential (E)","Each ion's \"favourite voltage\"; Vm moves toward it when that ion's channels open"],["","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","Electrochemical gradient"],["","What does the equilibrium potential assume about channels?","What does the equilibrium potential assume about channels?","All channels for that ion are open"]]},{"id":32074,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why does an action potential approach but not reach ENa (+60 mV)?","o":["Na⁺ channels inactivate quickly and K⁺ opens","ENa is actually +20 mV, so it does reach it","Na⁺ channels never open","Ca²⁺ dominates","The pump reverses"],"e":"The AP peak is +20 to +40 mV. Na⁺ channels open only very briefly then inactivate, and delayed rectifier K⁺ channels open, so Vm turns back before reaching +60.","w":["ENa is +60.","They open.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Peak below ENa.","rn":[["","How fast do Na+ channels inactivate after opening in an AP (action potential)?","How fast do Na+ channels inactivate after opening in an AP (action potential)?","Within a fraction of a millisecond"]]},{"id":32075,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why is the after-hyperpolarisation close to EK?","o":["K⁺ permeability is high and Na⁺ low","Na⁺ permeability is high and K⁺ low","The pump stops","Ca²⁺ channels are open","Cl⁻ floods in"],"e":"During AHP, ↑ K⁺ and ↓ Na⁺ permeability; Vm approaches K⁺’s equilibrium potential and the neuron works hard to re-establish rest.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AHP near EK.","rn":[["","Late in an AP (action potential), how does the conductance ratio shift?","Late in an AP (action potential), how does the conductance ratio shift?","Towards high K+ conductance; Low Na+ conductance: K+ lost, almost no Na+ enters"]]},{"id":32076,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"A neuron rests at −70 mV and ECl in neurons is −65 mV. If Cl⁻ channels open, how does Vm move?","o":["Slightly towards −65 mV","Strongly towards +60 mV","To −90 mV","To 0 mV","Towards +123 mV"],"e":"Opening channels for an ion moves Vm towards that ion’s equilibrium potential. ECl in neurons is −65 mV, close to rest, so Vm moves slightly towards −65.","w":["ENa.","EK.","Not.","ECa."],"k":"Cl⁻ → towards ECl.","rn":[["","Equilibrium potential (E)","Equilibrium potential (E)","Each ion's \"favourite voltage\"; Vm moves toward it when that ion's channels open"]]},{"id":32077,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Opening Ca²⁺ channels alone would drive Vm towards which value?","o":["+123 mV","−90 mV, as Ca²⁺ is 2+ and leaves","−40 mV","+60 mV","0 mV"],"e":"ECa is +123 mV – calcium is 2+, with extra charge. Opening only Ca²⁺ channels drives Vm towards +123 mV.","w":["EK.","ECl.","ENa.","Not."],"k":"ECa +123.","rn":[["","What is ECa?","What is ECa?","+123 mV"]]},{"id":32078,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A neuron with many leak K⁺ channels and few Na⁺ leak channels will rest near:","o":["EK, very negative","ENa, positive","The average of ENa and EK","ECa","0 mV"],"e":"If one channel type is much more conductive than all others, Vm becomes nearly that ion’s E. Many K⁺ leak channels → Vm near EK.","w":["Few Na⁺.","Needs equal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ leaks → near EK.","rn":[["","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","K+ going out of the cell; Near EK"]]},{"id":32079,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why are Na⁺ channels described as working with “military precision”?","o":["They open and close very quickly and briefly","They open slowly and stay open a long time, ensuring repolarisation","They never close","They need ATP","They are ligand-gated"],"e":"Na⁺ channels open and close with “military precision” (tight peak); K⁺ channels are slow and sluggish – giving the “beautiful” timing of K⁺ opening slightly after Na⁺.","w":["That is K⁺.","They close.","Not.","Voltage-gated."],"k":"Na⁺: fast, precise.","rn":[["","Na+ channels vs K+ channels: speed of opening?","Na+ channels vs K+ channels: speed of opening?","Na+: fast, open briefly then close; K+: slow, sluggish (\"delayed\")"]]},{"id":32080,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why is the slightly delayed opening of K⁺ channels useful?","o":["It lets Na⁺ depolarise first, then repolarise","It prevents depolarisation from ever starting","It keeps Vm at 0 mV","It causes neurotransmitter release","It is harmful"],"e":"You don’t want K⁺ channels to open straight away, but slightly after Na⁺ – this timing allows the upstroke then repolarisation.","w":["Not.","Not.","Ca²⁺ does.","Useful."],"k":"K⁺ delay: timing."},{"id":32081,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A neurotransmitter opens Na⁺-permeable ligand-gated channels on a post-synaptic neuron. What follows?","o":["Depolarisation, perhaps reaching threshold","Hyperpolarisation, moving Vm away from threshold","Immediate neurotransmitter release from that cell","No change","Closure of Ca²⁺ channels"],"e":"If the receptor opens a Na⁺ channel, it causes depolarisation in the post-synaptic cell; enough of that and you get an AP. Initial depolarisation can come from synaptic transmission via a ligand-gated channel.","w":["Cl⁻ would.","Needs an AP first.","It changes.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ ligand-gated → depolarise.","rn":[["","How does the AP (action potential) reach the presynaptic terminal to start release?","How does the AP (action potential) reach the presynaptic terminal to start release?","Travels down the neuron; Depolarises the terminal"],["","What are the 4 steps of an action potential?","What are the 4 steps of an action potential?","Resting stage; Slight depolarisation to threshold; Na+ conductance shoots up (depolarisation); Na+ falls, K+ rises (repolarisation)"]]},{"id":32082,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Name the phase at <b>D</b>, where Vm dips below the resting level after the spike.","e":"D is the after-hyperpolarisation: slow K⁺ channels stay open (↑K⁺, ↓Na⁺ permeability), so Vm approaches EK; this overlaps the relative refractory period.","k":"D = after-hyperpolarisation.","img":{"src":"32/ap","cap":"Neuronal membrane potential over time after a stimulus. Some labels removed."},"a":["after-hyperpolarisation","afterhyperpolarisation","hyperpolarisation","hyperpolarization","after-hyperpolarization","ahp","undershoot"],"rn":[["","What is the positive afterpotential?","What is the positive afterpotential?","The dip below resting Vm after the AP; Same period as the after-hyperpolarisation (AHP)"]]},{"id":32083,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why is changing only a thin layer of membrane efficient for long axons?","o":["Changing the whole cell interior would need far too many ions","The axon has no cytoplasm","Ions cannot move within the cell","It uses ATP only","It is not efficient"],"e":"Only the thin layer just inside the membrane changes, not the whole cell. Changing the whole inside would need far too much ion exchange; you just need a conductance change along the membrane.","w":["It does.","They can.","Not.","It is."],"k":"Thin layer efficient.","rn":[["","Why is a whole-cell charge change not needed for propagation?","Why is a whole-cell charge change not needed for propagation?","Only the inner membrane layer changes; Less ion exchange"]]},{"id":32084,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which feature of an AP is caused by positive feedback?","o":["The rapid upstroke after threshold","The after-hyperpolarisation","The resting potential","The relative refractory period","Ca²⁺ removal"],"e":"Positive feedback: a few Na⁺ channels open → Vm rises → more Na⁺ channels open … “boom” – the rapid depolarisation upstroke.","w":["K⁺.","K⁺ leak.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Upstroke: positive feedback."},{"id":32085,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which pair of values best fits a neuron at rest and at the AP peak?","o":["−70 mV and +30 mV","−15 mV and +123 mV","0 mV and +60 mV","−90 mV and −50 mV","+20 mV and −70 mV"],"e":"Rest −70 to −90 mV; AP peak +20 to +40 mV.","w":["Not.","Not.","Threshold is −50.","Reversed."],"k":"Rest −70; peak +30.","rn":[["","Action potential: rest and peak voltages","Action potential: rest and peak voltages","Rest: -70 to -90 mV; Peak: +20 to +40 mV"]]},{"id":32086,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which ion movement “clamps” the membrane negative at rest?","o":["K⁺ leaving the cell","Na⁺ entering the cell","Ca²⁺ entering","Cl⁻ leaving","K⁺ entering"],"e":"Resting stage: K⁺ going out of the cell clamps the membrane potential negative (near EK), with greater K⁺ permeability.","w":["Depolarises.","Depolarises.","Not.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ out clamps negative.","rn":[["","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","At the resting stage, what clamps Vm negative?","K+ going out of the cell; Near EK"]]},{"id":32087,"t":"Action Potential","s":"What usually provides the initial depolarisation of a neuron?","o":["A nearby cell or synaptic transmission","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump reversing","Spontaneous K⁺ influx","Ca²⁺ leaving","Vesicle fusion"],"e":"‘Something’ makes the cell less negative: a nearby cell depolarising, or synaptic transmission where a neurotransmitter opens a ligand-gated channel.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Presynaptic."],"k":"Initial depolarisation source.","rn":[["","What are 2 causes of the initial depolarisation?","What are 2 causes of the initial depolarisation?","Nearby cell depolarising; Neurotransmitter opening a ligand-gated channel"]]},{"id":32088,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A neurotransmitter binds a metabotropic receptor. How might this still change the membrane potential?","o":["cAMP can activate other channels","The receptor itself is a channel that opens","It cannot affect Vm","It releases Ca²⁺ into the cleft","It blocks the pump"],"e":"5b metabotropic: an intracellular cascade (G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP), which can in turn activate other channels.","w":["That is ionotropic.","It can.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Metabotropic → cAMP → channels.","rn":[["","What can a metabotropic receptor signalling cascade go on to do?","What can a metabotropic receptor signalling cascade go on to do?","Activate other ion-selective channels"]]},{"id":32089,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which best explains why a muscle could not work properly if ACh were never cleared?","o":["Signals would no longer be proportional to impulses","ACh would be converted into noradrenaline","Na⁺ channels would close permanently","Ca²⁺ would never enter","Vesicles would never fuse"],"e":"Termination must be proportional: one impulse → one release → one cycle. If ACh were not cleared there would be a non-proportional signal to muscles, so walking would be impossible or very challenging.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Uncleared ACh: non-proportional.","rn":[["","Why must neurotransmitter be cleared?","Why must neurotransmitter be cleared?","Signal must be proportional; One impulse, one release"]]},{"id":32090,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"What is the role of synaptic vesicles?","o":["They store transmitter until Ca²⁺ triggers release","They make ATP for the pump","They are receptors","They degrade transmitter","They generate the AP"],"e":"Synaptic vesicles (“wee bags” of transmitter) store it until needed; a local rise in Ca²⁺ makes them merge with the membrane and release.","w":["Not.","Not.","Enzymes do.","Na⁺ channels."],"k":"Vesicles store transmitter.","rn":[["","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","Increase in cytosolic free Ca2+"]]},{"id":32092,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Is ENa (+60 mV) a voltage on the inner or the outer face of the membrane?","o":["It is a difference across the membrane, like Vm","It is the voltage of the outer membrane only","It is the voltage of the nucleus","It is the resting Vm","It is the threshold"],"e":"It is the same concept as Vm – the difference across the membrane – but it is the equilibrium potential of that one ion, not the resting Vm.","w":["Difference.","Not.","Not resting.","Not."],"k":"E is a transmembrane difference.","rn":[["","What does ΔV stand for?","What does ΔV stand for?","Voltage difference across 2 points"],["","Membrane potential (Vm)","Membrane potential (Vm)","Overall electrical potential energy across the membrane"]]},{"id":32093,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Why is ENa positive while EK is negative?","o":["Na⁺ moves in and K⁺ moves out","Na⁺ is negatively charged","K⁺ is more abundant outside","Na⁺ channels are ligand-gated","They are both zero"],"e":"ENa is positive because Na⁺ moves the other way – into the cell, making the inside more positive. K⁺ moves out, making it negative.","w":["Na⁺ is positive.","Inside.","Voltage-gated.","Not."],"k":"Direction sets sign.","rn":[["","Why is ENa positive?","Why is ENa positive?","Na+ moves into the cell; Makes inside more positive"]]},{"id":32094,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Why does K⁺ diffusion alone give about −94 mV?","o":["The K⁺ inside:outside ratio is 35:1","The K⁺ inside:outside ratio is 1:35","The pump adds −94 mV","Na⁺ leaks give −94 mV","Ca²⁺ gives −94 mV"],"e":"K⁺ inside:outside ratio 35:1 gives a Nernst potential of −94 mV (log 35 = 1.54; 1.54 × −61 mV). If K⁺ were the only factor, rest would be −94 mV.","w":["Reversed.","Pump −4.","Na⁺ +61.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ 35:1 → −94 mV.","rn":[["","K+ inside to outside ratio and its Nernst potential?","K+ inside to outside ratio and its Nernst potential?","35 : 1; -94 mV"]]},{"id":32095,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Early in the AP, the Na⁺:K⁺ conductance ratio rises more than 1000-fold. What results?","o":["Far more Na⁺ flows in than K⁺ out; Vm goes positive","Far more K⁺ flows out; Vm goes very negative","No ion flow","Ca²⁺ dominates","The pump stops"],"e":"Early on, the Na⁺:K⁺ conductance ratio rises >1000-fold, so far more Na⁺ flows in than K⁺ out and the membrane becomes positive. Then Na⁺ channels close and K⁺ channels open.","w":["Later.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ dominates early.","rn":[["","Na+ channels open: which way does Na+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Na+ channels open: which way does Na+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Into the cell; Inside becomes positive"]]},{"id":32096,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Name the always-open K⁺ channels that explain the high resting K⁺ conductance.","e":"K⁺ leak channels make the resting membrane K⁺-permeable.","k":"K⁺ leak channels.","a":["leak channels","leak k+ channels","k+ leak channels","potassium leak channels","leak","two-pore channels","leak channel"],"rn":[["","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","50 to 100 times greater; Due to leak channels"],["","Why does Na+ still contribute a little to the resting potential?","Why does Na+ still contribute a little to the resting potential?","Slight Na+ permeability; Through K+-Na+ leak channels"]]},{"id":32097,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A neuron is exposed to a drug that makes its Na⁺ channels unable to inactivate. What would you predict?","o":["Repolarisation is impaired and depolarisation prolonged","APs become shorter and smaller","Vm becomes more negative at rest","Ca²⁺ release stops","Nothing"],"e":"Repolarisation needs Na⁺ channel inactivation plus delayed K⁺ opening. Without inactivation, Na⁺ keeps flowing in, impairing repolarisation and prolonging depolarisation.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","Not stated.","It changes."],"k":"No Na⁺ inactivation → prolonged.","rn":[["","What are the 4 steps of an action potential?","What are the 4 steps of an action potential?","Resting stage; Slight depolarisation to threshold; Na+ conductance shoots up (depolarisation); Na+ falls, K+ rises (repolarisation)"]]},{"id":32098,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why must the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase work hard after an action potential?","o":["To restore the Na⁺ and K⁺ gradients","To generate the next action potential directly","To release neurotransmitter","To open Ca²⁺ channels","To inactivate Na⁺ channels"],"e":"During and after the AP, the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase works hard to re-establish the gradients that set Vm.","w":["Not.","Ca²⁺ does.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pump restores gradients."},{"id":32099,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which statement about Ca²⁺ channels at the presynaptic terminal is correct?","o":["They are localised where the synapse is","They are spread evenly along the whole axon","They are ligand-gated","They let Ca²⁺ out","They are absent"],"e":"The Ca²⁺ channels are more localised where the synapse is, giving a localised pulse of Ca²⁺ whose only job is to release neurotransmitter.","w":["Not.","Voltage-gated.","In.","Present."],"k":"Localised Ca²⁺ channels.","rn":[["","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Very localised; Where neurotransmitter is released"]]},{"id":32100,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which of these is NOT a step of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","o":["Transport of the transmitter in the bloodstream","Synthesis in the soma or terminal","Storage in vesicles","Ca²⁺-triggered vesicle fusion and release","Diffusion across the cleft"],"e":"Life cycle: synthesis, storage, depolarisation and Ca²⁺ channel activation, vesicle fusion, release, diffusion, action on post-synaptic receptors, termination. Transmitters do not circulate in blood.","w":["Step.","Step.","Step.","Step."],"k":"Life cycle steps."},{"id":32101,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A drug prevents vesicle fusion at the terminal. What would happen?","o":["Release would fail despite APs and Ca²⁺ entry","More transmitter would be released","APs could not reach the terminal","Receptors would be blocked","Reuptake would increase"],"e":"Increased Ca²⁺ stimulates vesicle fusion and release. Blocking fusion stops release even though the AP and Ca²⁺ influx occur. (Botox was given as a drug acting on the cycle.)","w":["Less.","They can.","Not.","Not."],"k":"No fusion → no release."},{"id":32102,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which ion is the main determinant of the resting membrane potential?","e":"K⁺ leak channels make resting K⁺ conductance 50–100× that of Na⁺, so Vm rests near EK.","k":"K⁺.","a":["k+","k","potassium","k⁺","potassium ion"],"rn":[["","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","At rest, how does K+ conductance compare to Na+ conductance?","50 to 100 times greater; Due to leak channels"]]},{"id":32103,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Which ion entering the cell drives the upstroke of the action potential?","e":"Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open: Na⁺ entry drives depolarisation by positive feedback.","k":"Na⁺.","a":["na+","na","sodium","na⁺","sodium ion"],"rn":[["","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","Electrochemical gradient"]]},{"id":32104,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"Which ion is the main driver of repolarisation?","o":["K⁺ leaving","Na⁺ entering","Ca²⁺ entering","Cl⁻ leaving","K⁺ entering"],"e":"To repolarise, delayed rectifier K⁺ channels open and K⁺ rushes out down its gradient.","w":["Upstroke.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Repolarisation: K⁺ out.","rn":[["","Why does the inside get more negative as K+ leaves?","Why does the inside get more negative as K+ leaves?","Each K+ carries a positive charge out"],["","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","K+ leaving the cell: what 2 forces reach a balance?","Concentration gradient pushing out; Negative charge inside pulling back"],["","What is the Na+ to K+ conductance ratio doing early in an AP (action potential)?","What is the Na+ to K+ conductance ratio doing early in an AP (action potential)?","Rises more than 1000-fold; Far more Na+ enters than K+ leaves"]]},{"id":32105,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which ion entering the presynaptic terminal triggers neurotransmitter release?","e":"Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open at the terminal; Ca²⁺ triggers vesicle fusion.","k":"Ca²⁺.","a":["ca2+","ca","calcium","ca²⁺","calcium ion","ca++"],"rn":[["","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","What triggers neurotransmitter release?","Increase in cytosolic free Ca2+"],["","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","Synthesis and storage; Depolarisation, Ca2+ channels open; Vesicle transport, fusion, release; Diffusion across cleft; Action on post-synaptic cell; Signal termination"]]},{"id":32107,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A drug reduces the number of open K⁺ leak channels at rest. What happens to Vm?","o":["It becomes less negative, closer to threshold","It becomes more negative, further from threshold","It becomes exactly −90 mV","It stays the same","It becomes +60 mV"],"e":"Rest is near EK because K⁺ leak dominates. Reducing K⁺ conductance means relatively more Na⁺ influence, so Vm becomes less negative.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","It changes.","Not."],"k":"↓ K⁺ leak → less negative.","rn":[["","Depolarisation","Depolarisation","Inside voltage becomes less negative (more positive)"],["","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","K+ channels open: which way does K+ flow and what happens to the inside?","Out of the cell; Inside becomes negative"],["","Repolarisation","Repolarisation","Inside voltage becomes less positive (more negative); Returns to resting potential"]]},{"id":32108,"t":"Action Potential","s":"In which directions can an action potential travel on an excitable membrane?","o":["It travels in all directions from the stimulus","It travels only towards the stimulus","It never leaves the stimulus point","It travels only in myelin","It travels only in the cleft"],"e":"An excitable membrane has no single direction: the AP travels in all directions away from the stimulus, even along all branches. In real axons, Na⁺ inactivation keeps it going one way.","w":["Not.","It spreads.","Not.","Not."],"k":"All directions on a membrane.","rn":[["","In an excitable membrane, which direction does the AP travel from a stimulus?","In an excitable membrane, which direction does the AP travel from a stimulus?","All directions; Until the whole membrane is depolarised"]]},{"id":32109,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why does a fully permeable membrane relate to death?","o":["No gradient can be held, so Vm is zero","It causes very high Vm","It blocks ATP","It releases transmitter","It does not"],"e":"Fully permeable membranes generally have a Vm of zero (e.g. death): ions cross freely, so no voltage can be generated.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","It does."],"k":"Death: Vm zero."},{"id":32110,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Neurons use voltage changes to process information. Which sequence shows this?","o":["Vm changes → AP → next neuron’s Vm changes","Hormone → blood → Vm","DNA → RNA → Vm","ATP → ADP → AP","Vm stays constant"],"e":"A neuron has a Vm; changing it gives information; then maybe there is an AP and the next neuron’s Vm changes – “information processing, basically”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","It changes."],"k":"Information processing.","rn":[["","Synapse","Synapse","Junction point from one neuron to the next; How a neuron communicates with neurons or other cells"]]},{"id":32111,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Which statement is TRUE?","o":["Repolarisation occurs when delayed rectifier K⁺ channels open","Sodium is higher inside the cell","Depolarisation occurs when Na⁺ leaves","Transmitters circulate in blood","Na⁺ influx triggers release"],"e":"True: repolarisation happens when delayed rectifier K⁺ channels open (“a really polite way of saying lazy”). The rest are false.","w":["Outside.","Enters.","Not.","Ca²⁺."],"k":"Delayed rectifier.","rn":[["","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","Na+ channel inactivation; Delayed rectifier K+ channels open"],["","Delayed rectifier K+ channels opening has what effect?","Delayed rectifier K+ channels opening has what effect?","More K+ going out"]]},{"id":32112,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which statement about hormones relative to neurotransmitters is correct?","o":["Hormones are not neurotransmitters but can mimic them","Hormones are a type of neurotransmitter","Hormones never act on receptors","Hormones are released into the cleft","Hormones are exogenous drugs"],"e":"Not neurotransmitters: drugs (though they can mimic) and hormones; the example given was adrenaline.","w":["Not.","They do.","Blood.","Endogenous."],"k":"Hormones mimic.","rn":[["","What 2 things are NOT neurotransmitters?","What 2 things are NOT neurotransmitters?","Drugs (can mimic); Hormones"]]},{"id":32113,"t":"Action Potential","s":"Why are K⁺ and Na⁺ the two main ions for neuronal information?","o":["Their opposite gradients drive Vm changes","They are the only ions in cells","They are uncharged","They are both higher outside","They never cross membranes"],"e":"K⁺ (high inside) and Na⁺ (high outside) are the two main ions; channels for each move Vm towards their very different equilibrium potentials.","w":["Not.","Charged.","K⁺ inside.","They do."],"k":"K⁺ and Na⁺: main ions."},{"id":32114,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which statement about the AP and second messengers is correct?","o":["The AP causes other changes, e.g. second messengers","The AP never affects anything except Na⁺","Second messengers start the AP","APs only change K⁺","APs cause DNA changes"],"e":"The AP leads to other changes inside the cell (e.g. second messengers); most important, Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ influx increases.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AP → other changes.","rn":[["","What does an AP lead to inside the cell?","What does an AP lead to inside the cell?","Other changes, e.g. second messengers"]]},{"id":32115,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A neuron is depolarised to just above threshold. Compared with a much larger depolarisation, what is the AP size?","o":["The same – all-or-none","Smaller, as the stimulus is weaker","Larger","No AP","Twice as large"],"e":"APs are all-or-none: once committed above threshold, the AP runs fully regardless of how far above threshold the stimulus is.","w":["Not graded.","Not.","It fires.","Not."],"k":"All-or-none size.","rn":[["","What kind of response is an AP (action potential)?","What kind of response is an AP (action potential)?","\"All-or-none\""]]},{"id":32116,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Where do neurotransmitters act besides other neurons?","o":["Myocytes and endocrine cells","Only on other neurons","Only in blood","Only on DNA","Only on bone"],"e":"They signal to other neurons, myocytes (e.g. at the neuromuscular junction) and endocrine cells.","w":["Not only.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Targets: neurons, myocytes, endocrine.","rn":[["","What are the 4 features of a neurotransmitter?","What are the 4 features of a neurotransmitter?","Endogenous chemical; Released extracellularly by a neuron; Signals to neurons, myocytes, endocrine cells; Under physiological conditions"],["","Synapses connect neurons to which other cells/tissues?","Synapses connect neurons to which other cells/tissues?","Muscle; Endocrine cells"]]},{"id":32117,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A drug makes K⁺ channels open at the same time as Na⁺ channels. What would happen to the AP?","o":["The upstroke would be reduced or prevented","The AP would be larger, as both ions push Vm the same way","No effect","Ca²⁺ release would rise","The resting Vm would rise"],"e":"The timing matters: K⁺ opening slightly after Na⁺ lets the upstroke happen. K⁺ efflux at the same time would oppose depolarisation, reducing or preventing the upstroke.","w":["Opposite directions.","It matters.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Timing matters."},{"id":32118,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"Which best summarises how ionic conductance relates to Vm?","o":["Vm reflects which ions are permeable and their E","Vm is fixed regardless of ion channels","Vm depends only on ATP","Vm depends only on Ca²⁺","Vm depends on hormones only"],"e":"The voltage across the membrane depends on which ions are currently permeable; ionic conductance and currents determine Vm, depending on each ion and its equilibrium potential.","w":["It changes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Conductance determines Vm.","rn":[["","The voltage across the membrane depends on what?","The voltage across the membrane depends on what?","Which ions are currently permeable"]]},{"id":32119,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which receptors at the post-synaptic membrane can be activated by a transmitter?","o":["Any receptor type, ionotropic or metabotropic","Only GPCRs","Only nuclear receptors","Only Na⁺/K⁺ pumps","None"],"e":"Action on the post-synaptic cell: activation of specific receptors, excitatory or inhibitory; open ion channel; any of the different types of receptors (5a ionotropic, 5b metabotropic).","w":["Not only.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Any receptor type.","rn":[["","What are the 2 post-synaptic receptor types a neurotransmitter binds?","What are the 2 post-synaptic receptor types a neurotransmitter binds?","Ionotropic; Metabotropic"]]},{"id":32120,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Which best summarises neuronal communication?","o":["Ion channels give an AP, which releases transmitter","Hormones in blood generate APs","Neurons communicate without ions","ATP crosses synapses","DNA is transmitted"],"e":"Sequential opening of ion channels leads to an AP; the AP causes release of a neurotransmitter via voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and Ca²⁺ influx – electrochemical events.","w":["Not.","Ions essential.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Neuronal communication summary.","rn":[["","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Where are presynaptic Ca2+ channels located?","Very localised; Where neurotransmitter is released"],["","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","What drives ion flow through an open channel?","Electrochemical gradient"],["","Equilibrium potential (E)","Equilibrium potential (E)","Each ion's \"favourite voltage\"; Vm moves toward it when that ion's channels open"]]},{"id":32121,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"In an experiment on a neuron, a drug opens a very large number of one type of ion channel so that this ion's conductance far exceeds all others. The membrane potential moves from rest to about +60 mV. In a second experiment, a different drug opens only another channel type, and the membrane moves to about −90 mV.<br><br>Which channels were opened, and why does the membrane reach these values?","o":["Na⁺ then K⁺; Vm moves to the equilibrium potential of the dominant ion","K⁺ then Na⁺; Vm moves away from the ion's equilibrium potential","Ca²⁺ then Cl⁻; their equilibrium potentials are +60 and −90 mV","Cl⁻ then Na⁺; the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase sets Vm","Na⁺ then K⁺; Vm always stays at the resting potential whichever channel opens"],"e":"Step 1: each ion has an equilibrium potential (ENa +60 mV, EK −90 mV, ECa +123 mV, ECl −40 mV, or −65 mV in neurons), and when its channels open, Vm moves towards that value. Step 2: if one channel type is much more conductive than all others, Vm becomes nearly that ion's equilibrium potential, so +60 mV means Na⁺ and −90 mV means K⁺.","w":["The ions are swapped, and Vm moves towards E.","ECa is +123 mV and ECl is −40/−65 mV.","The values match ENa and EK, not Cl⁻.","Vm changes when ion conductance changes."],"k":"Vm moves towards E of the dominant ion: ENa +60, EK −90.","rn":[["","Equilibrium potential (E)","Equilibrium potential (E)","Each ion's \"favourite voltage\"; Vm moves toward it when that ion's channels open"],["","One ion channel type far more conductive than all others: what is Vm?","One ion channel type far more conductive than all others: what is Vm?","(Nearly) that ion's equilibrium potential"],["","What are the equilibrium potentials?","What are the equilibrium potentials?","ENa: +60 mV; EK: -90 mV; ECa: +123 mV; ECl: -40 mV (-65 in neurons)"]]},{"id":32122,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A researcher gives a neuron two small electrical stimuli. Stimulus A depolarises it slightly, but the membrane potential stays below about −65 mV and quickly returns to rest. Stimulus B depolarises it a little further, past this point, and a full action potential fires, with the same size as any other action potential in that neuron.<br><br>Which statement best explains these results?","o":["A was below threshold (failed initiation); B passed threshold, triggering all-or-none positive feedback","A caused a small action potential; B caused a larger graded one","Both fired action potentials of different sizes","A was in the absolute refractory period; B was in a relative refractory period with K⁺ entering the cell","B opened K⁺ channels, which depolarise the membrane"],"e":"Step 1: if Vm stays below threshold, background K⁺ permeability pulls it back to rest: a failed initiation. Step 2: above threshold (−65 to −50 mV), a positive feedback loop of Na⁺ channel opening and depolarisation commits the cell to an action potential, which is all-or-none.","w":["Action potentials are all-or-none, not graded.","A did not fire an action potential.","No previous action potential is described, and K⁺ leaves the cell.","Na⁺ entry, not K⁺, depolarises the membrane."],"k":"Below threshold = failed initiation; above = all-or-none AP.","rn":[["","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Vm stays below threshold: what happens?","Background K+ permeability pulls Vm back to rest; \"Failed initiation\""]]},{"id":32123,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A toxin delays the closing of sodium channels in a neuron and also blocks the potassium channels that normally open after a delay. Action potentials still start, but the membrane stays depolarised for much longer than normal before returning to rest.<br><br>Which two events normally cause repolarisation?","o":["Na⁺ channel inactivation and opening of delayed rectifier K⁺ channels","Ca²⁺ channel opening and Cl⁻ influx","Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase activity alone","Opening of more Na⁺ channels and closing of K⁺ channels","Ligand-gated channel opening by a neurotransmitter, which makes the inside of the cell positive"],"e":"Step 1: repolarisation makes the membrane less positive inside. Step 2: it is caused by two delayed-action events: Na⁺ channel inactivation (less Na⁺ entering) and opening of delayed rectifier K⁺ channels (more K⁺ leaving); the toxin blocks both, so repolarisation is delayed.","w":["Ca²⁺ opening and Cl⁻ are not the repolarisation events described.","The pump contributes to rest, but repolarisation is due to channels.","More Na⁺ entry would depolarise further.","Ligand-gated opening causes depolarisation."],"k":"Repolarisation: Na⁺ inactivation + delayed rectifier K⁺ opening.","rn":[["","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","What are the 2 delayed-action events causing repolarisation?","Na+ channel inactivation; Delayed rectifier K+ channels open"]]},{"id":32124,"t":"Action Potential","s":"A researcher stimulates a nerve fibre twice. When the second stimulus comes immediately after an action potential, no new action potential can be generated however strong it is. A little later, a second action potential can be triggered, but only by a stronger than normal stimulus.<br><br>What are these two periods, and what causes the first?","o":["Absolute then relative refractory period; the first is due to inactivated Na⁺ channels","Relative then absolute refractory period; caused by Ca²⁺ influx","Failed initiation then threshold; caused by Cl⁻ channels","After-hyperpolarisation then depolarisation; caused by the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase, which pumps Na⁺ back in","Absolute refractory period only; caused by excess K⁺ entering"],"e":"Step 1: during after-hyperpolarisation, K⁺ permeability is raised and Na⁺ permeability lowered, and an absolute refractory period occurs in which new action potentials cannot be initiated because sodium channels are inactivated. Step 2: a relative refractory period follows, in which an action potential can be started only with a higher than normal voltage to activate the resting sodium channels.","w":["The order is reversed.","These are not refractory periods.","The pump moves Na⁺ out, not in, and is not the cause.","There are two periods, and K⁺ leaves the cell."],"k":"Absolute RP (inactivated Na⁺ channels) → relative RP (stronger stimulus needed).","rn":[["","Absolute refractory period","Absolute refractory period","New APs cannot be initiated; Na+ channels inactivated"]]},{"id":32125,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A researcher removes calcium from the fluid around a synapse. Action potentials still reach the presynaptic terminal and depolarise it normally, but no neurotransmitter is released and the postsynaptic cell does not respond.<br><br>Why does transmission fail?","o":["Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, but no Ca²⁺ enters to trigger vesicle fusion and release","Na⁺ channels cannot open without calcium","The postsynaptic receptors need calcium to bind the transmitter","Calcium is needed to synthesise all neurotransmitters in the cell body","Without calcium, K⁺ cannot leave the presynaptic cell, so the action potential never reaches the terminal"],"e":"Step 1: when Vm becomes positive during the action potential, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ flows in. Step 2: the rise in cytosolic free calcium triggers vesicle fusion and release of the neurotransmitter; with no extracellular calcium, there is no influx and no release.","w":["Action potentials still occur, so Na⁺ channels work.","The problem is release, not postsynaptic binding.","Synthesis is not what fails here.","The action potential does reach the terminal."],"k":"AP → voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels → Ca²⁺ influx → transmitter release.","rn":[["","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","Synthesis and storage; Depolarisation, Ca2+ channels open; Vesicle transport, fusion, release; Diffusion across cleft; Action on post-synaptic cell; Signal termination"],["","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Calcium channels; (Voltage-gated Ca2+)"]]},{"id":32126,"t":"Electrical Definitions","s":"A physiologist measures current through a set of ion channels. When she doubles the number of open channels (doubling the conductance) at the same driving voltage, the current doubles. When she halves the voltage with the same conductance, the current halves.<br><br>Which relationship explains this?","o":["I = V × g (Ohm's law; g = 1/R)","I = V / g","V = I / R","g = V × R","I = R × g, which means current depends only on the resistance of the membrane"],"e":"Step 1: Ohm's law states V = I × R, and conductance g is the inverse of resistance (g = 1/R). Step 2: rearranged, I = V × g: multiplying the force on an ion (voltage) by the ability to conduct it (conductance) gives the current, so doubling either doubles the current.","w":["Current is V × g, not V / g.","V = I × R, not I / R.","g = 1/R.","Current depends on voltage and conductance."],"k":"Ohm's law: V = IR; I = Vg (g = 1/R).","rn":[["","What is Ohm's Law?","What is Ohm's Law?","V = I x R"],["","What is the other form of Ohm's Law using conductance?","What is the other form of Ohm's Law using conductance?","I = V x g"]]},{"id":32127,"t":"Membrane Potential and Ion Channels","s":"A neuron at rest has a membrane potential of about −80 mV. When a researcher blocks its background potassium channels, the membrane becomes less negative. When the cell is killed and its membrane becomes fully permeable, the membrane potential falls to zero.<br><br>What contributes to the resting potential, and why does a dead cell have no potential?","o":["K⁺ and Na⁺ diffusion potentials and the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase; a fully permeable membrane has Vm of zero","Only Ca²⁺ diffusion; dead cells have Vm of +60 mV","Only the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase; dead cells keep their gradients","Cl⁻ entry only; dead cells become hyperpolarised","Na⁺ channels being fully open at rest; dead cells have a resting potential of −90 mV"],"e":"Step 1: the resting membrane potential (about −70 to −90 mV) is made up of the sodium and potassium diffusion potentials and the Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase; at rest the membrane is more permeable to K⁺, so blocking K⁺ channels makes Vm less negative. Step 2: Vm depends on selective permeability, and fully permeable membranes (as in death) generally have a Vm of zero.","w":["Ca²⁺ is not the resting contributor, and dead cells have zero Vm.","Diffusion potentials also contribute, and dead cells lose gradients.","K⁺ and Na⁺ diffusion and the pump set the resting potential.","The membrane at rest is more permeable to K⁺, not Na⁺."],"k":"Rest: K⁺/Na⁺ diffusion + Na⁺–K⁺ ATPase; fully permeable = 0 mV.","rn":[["","What is the Vm of a fully permeable membrane?","What is the Vm of a fully permeable membrane?","Zero (generally); e.g. death"],["","What are the 3 contributors to the resting membrane potential?","What are the 3 contributors to the resting membrane potential?","Sodium diffusion potential; Potassium diffusion potential; Na+-K+ ATPase"]]},{"id":32128,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A patient with depression is given a drug that stops a neurotransmitter being taken back into the presynaptic neuron. A different patient is given a botulinum toxin injection that stops vesicles fusing and releasing their contents. The lecturer notes that each step of the transmitter life cycle is a drug target.<br><br>Which steps do these two drugs act on?","o":["First: signal termination by reuptake; second: vesicle fusion and release","First: synthesis; second: storage","First: receptor activation; second: diffusion across the cleft","First: release; second: reuptake","Both act on the postsynaptic receptor, which is the only drug target in a synapse"],"e":"Step 1: the life cycle is synthesis, storage in vesicles, depolarisation and Ca²⁺ channel activation, vesicle fusion and release, diffusion across the cleft, action on postsynaptic receptors, and signal termination (removal, enzyme degradation or reuptake). Step 2: blocking reuptake acts on signal termination (as with antidepressants), and stopping vesicle fusion acts on release (as with botox).","w":["Neither drug acts on synthesis or storage.","Neither drug acts on receptors or diffusion.","The two steps are swapped.","Each step of the life cycle can be a drug target."],"k":"Each life-cycle step is a drug target: reuptake (antidepressants), release (botox).","rn":[["","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","What are the steps of the neurotransmitter life cycle?","Synthesis and storage; Depolarisation, Ca2+ channels open; Vesicle transport, fusion, release; Diffusion across cleft; Action on post-synaptic cell; Signal termination"]]},{"id":32129,"t":"Equilibrium and Resting Membrane Potential","s":"In a model cell, a researcher opens equal numbers of Na⁺ and K⁺ channels so that the membrane is exactly equally permeable to both ions, with no other currents. A student predicts that the membrane will sit at the Na⁺ equilibrium potential.<br><br>What value will the membrane potential actually reach?","o":["About −15 mV, the average of ENa (+60) and EK (−90)","+60 mV, the Na⁺ equilibrium potential","−90 mV, the K⁺ equilibrium potential","0 mV, as in a fully permeable dead cell","+123 mV, the calcium equilibrium potential, as both channels let calcium through as well"],"e":"Step 1: the membrane potential moves towards the equilibrium potential of each permeable ion in proportion to its conductance. Step 2: if the membrane is exactly equally permeable to Na⁺ (+60 mV) and K⁺ (−90 mV), Vm will be the average of their equilibrium potentials: −15 mV (an unlikely situation in real cells).","w":["Equal K⁺ permeability pulls Vm away from ENa.","Equal Na⁺ permeability pulls Vm away from EK.","The membrane is selectively permeable to only two ions.","Calcium channels are not open here."],"k":"Equal Na⁺/K⁺ permeability → average of E values (−15 mV).","rn":[["","Membrane equally permeable to Na+ and K+: Vm goes to?","Membrane equally permeable to Na+ and K+: Vm goes to?","-15 mV; Average of ENa and EK"]]},{"id":32130,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"At a synapse, an action potential in the presynaptic neuron causes release of a transmitter that diffuses across the cleft. It opens ligand-gated Na⁺ channels on the postsynaptic cell, which depolarises. The signal never travels backwards from the postsynaptic cell to the presynaptic one.<br><br>Which type of synapse is this, and why does the signal go only one way?","o":["A chemical synapse, which conducts in one direction only","An electrical synapse, which conducts one way only","A chemical synapse, which conducts both ways equally","A gap junction, which only opens in one direction","An electrical synapse, because ions flow directly between the cells through connexins"],"e":"Step 1: there are two types of synapse, chemical and electrical; in a chemical synapse, the electrical signal is converted to a chemical one (transmitter release), which opens receptors (e.g. ligand-gated Na⁺ channels) on the postsynaptic cell, causing depolarisation. Step 2: chemical synapses show one-way conduction, from the presynaptic neuron across the cleft to the postsynaptic cell.","w":["The synapse uses a transmitter, so it is chemical.","Chemical synapses conduct one way only.","A gap junction is an electrical connection, not a transmitter synapse.","Ions do not pass directly between the cells here."],"k":"Chemical synapse: transmitter-mediated, one-way conduction.","rn":[["","Which synapse type has one-way conduction?","Which synapse type has one-way conduction?","Chemical"],["","What are the 2 types of synapse?","What are the 2 types of synapse?","Chemical; Electrical"]]},{"id":32501,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"An action potential opens channel <b>6</b> at the presynaptic terminal. Which ion enters to trigger vesicle fusion at 7?","a":["calcium","ca2+","ca","ca²⁺","ca++"],"e":"6 is a voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channel; Ca²⁺ entry triggers fusion (7) and transmitter release into the cleft (4).","k":"Ca²⁺.","img":{"src":"32/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Most important: what opens when Vm becomes positive during an AP?","Calcium channels; (Voltage-gated Ca2+)"]]},{"id":32502,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"A toxin blocks channel <b>6</b>. Presynaptic action potentials are normal. What happens?","o":["Less transmitter is released, so the postsynaptic response falls","More transmitter is released, causing spasm","Transmitter stays longer in cleft 4","Postsynaptic receptors 5 open spontaneously","The presynaptic action potential cannot be generated"],"w":["Without Ca²⁺ entry, fusion falls.","That would follow blocking reuptake (8) or degradation.","Receptors still need transmitter.","Na⁺/K⁺ channels, not channel 6, make the AP."],"e":"Blocking presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels (as conotoxin does) reduces vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","k":"No Ca²⁺ entry → no release.","img":{"src":"32/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."}},{"id":32503,"t":"Synapses and Neurotransmitters","s":"Name the narrow gap labelled <b>4</b> into which transmitter is released.","a":["synaptic cleft","cleft","synaptic gap"],"e":"4 is the synaptic cleft; transmitter crosses it to bind postsynaptic receptors (5).","k":"4 = synaptic cleft.","img":{"src":"32/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","How can transmitter be degraded to end the signal?","By enzymes in the synaptic cleft"],["","What are the 3 parts of a synapse?","What are the 3 parts of a synapse?","Pre-synaptic; Synaptic cleft; Post-synaptic"],["","How wide is the synaptic cleft?","How wide is the synaptic cleft?","200-300 Å"]]}]);
